# Day 55

**53km (on canoe) + 5km (walking) / 11 hours**

*The Flying Fox to Whanganui*

Phew, that was a tough day. I can see why most canoe operators prefer to stop off at Pipiriki instead of doing what we do and dragging on to Whanganui. The first 10km or so was already a bit tough as the river is getting wider and the current less impactful requiring more powerful paddles to get the speed up. Gone are the days of racing down the river. Every rapid becomes a joy as it boosts you further down instead of being a cause for concern.

![The river from early in the day looking peaceful; a little too peaceful](./river.avif)

The wind was also a cause for struggle as it blowed all over and the headwinds or crosswinds are a pain to contend with as they blow the boat all over and make progress very difficult. It was incredibly demoralising when I was pulling very hard on the paddle but the water felt like cement and the boat was basically going nowhere.

After that we got to Hipango Park and decided to wait for a while to rest up and for the tides to be slightly more favourable. The park was nice but it would've been cool if they had a sign by the boat jetty to inform us to turn left to get to the park with a shelter, toilets and water. The facilities weren't great as two of the water taps didn't work and the toilet was absolutely covered in spider webs and plants made the door difficult to close but it was still nice to have. 

![The view of the river from near the shelter](./river-2
avif)

We decided to push on for another 5km even though the tide wasn't quite right yet. It was a hot day and sitting around wasn't very exciting. The ideal time to go is when the tide is going out so after high tide. High tide for us was mid afternoon which was a bit inconvenient since waiting for that would mean getting in quite late so there was incentive to push through the tide anyway. However this 5km may have been the toughest section of the whole river with the strong winds blowing the boat about and the tides pushing against us it just made every paddle require a huge effort from the arms and upper body to keep the boat moving forward. It was a huge relief to get past that and wait a bit longer. 

After all that the final section was just long. It felt mostly flat but you wanted to go faster to get off the river quicker but that required high effort and it dragged on and on and on. We had been given a paper map with instructions and interesting historical information about parts of the river and every curve and line on the map seemed to go on forever in real life. It didn't help that the river was extremely wide at this point. Eventually we came to the landing next to the holiday park where we repacked all our gear and left behind the canoes and related paraphernalia behind. 

![The boat ramp at the holiday park](./river-up.avif)

Finally there was a short and pretty basic walk along the sidewalk into Whanganui (or Wanganui if you prefer). Not much to say about this except that it felt good to be walking again, especially next to the river which had given me strife.



